{
 "duration": 57.456,
 "language": "en",
 "text": "In 1803, a giant escaped convict walked into the Australian bush and everyone assumed he was dead. William Buckley was about six foot six. He escaped a failed settlement on Port Phillip Bay and wandered alone, starving for weeks. Then the Wadawurrung people found him. He was carrying a spear taken from a grave and some believed he was a relative returned from the dead, so they took him in. And Buckley stayed. For 32 years. He learned their language, their laws, their country. He had a family. In 1835, settlers from Tasmania set up camp nearby and a huge sunburnt man walked out of the bush speaking almost no English. They thought he'd come from nowhere. Some say that's where the Aussie phrase Buckley's chance comes from, the chance of surviving like he did, which back in 1803 was basically none.",
